.TH CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT \- zero-initialiser for chttpsvr_config_t with sensible defaults
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

chttpsvr_config_t cfg = CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Expands to a compound literal that initialises a chttpsvr_config_t
with the following defaults:

  host                  = "0.0.0.0"
  port                  = 8080
  max_body_size         = 4 MiB
  read_timeout_ms       = 0   (no idle timeout; see below)
  idle_timeout_ms       = 0   (0 = fall back to read_timeout_ms)
  stream_read_timeout_ms = 30000   (30 s; 0 = wait indefinitely)
  max_body_read_duration_ms = 0   (disabled; no cap on total read time)
  response_write_timeout_ms = 0   (0 = use stream_read_timeout_ms's value)
  max_header_bytes     = 0   (0 = library default, 64 KiB)
  max_connections       = 0   (0 = unlimited)
  enable_keepalive      = false
  enable_reuseport      = false
  ipv6_only             = false
  tls                   = NULL
  worker_thread_count   = 0   (0 = CPU core count)
  worker_queue_capacity = 0   (0 = 1024 * worker_thread_count)

host may also be a "unix://path/to/socket" string, which binds a Unix
domain socket at that path instead of a TCP listener (port is then
ignored); a stale socket file already at that path is removed
automatically before binding.

read_timeout_ms/idle_timeout_ms are enforced by a module-local,
process-wide idle-timeout sweep thread (shared by every chttpsvr
instance), not by any per-connection OS timer: they bound how long a
connection may sit idle between requests (awaiting the next
pipelined/keep-alive request's headers), not how long a request
already being handled by a worker thread may take (see
max_body_read_duration_ms for that).

stream_read_timeout_ms bounds how long a worker thread will wait for
the *next* batch of body bytes while reading a request body (buffered
or streaming), independent of read_timeout_ms/idle_timeout_ms; those
reset on any connection activity and so do not protect against a
client that trickles bytes just fast enough to always beat them.  On
expiry, chttpsvr_req_read returns -1 and chttpsvr_req_stream_error
reports ccol_timed_out; buffered routes respond 408 automatically.

Note that stream_read_timeout_ms itself resets on any new byte too, so
a client that trickles a byte or two just before each gap expires
defeats it the same way it defeats read_timeout_ms/idle_timeout_ms.
max_body_read_duration_ms closes that loophole by separately capping
the *total* wall-clock time spent reading one request's body,
regardless of how many individual gaps it took to get there.  It is
disabled (0) by default so existing deployments are unaffected unless
they opt in.  On expiry it reports the same ccol_timed_out outcome as
a stream_read_timeout_ms expiry.

response_write_timeout_ms bounds how long a worker thread will wait,
per write(2)-equivalent call, while sending one response to a
slow-reading client; this protects the same limited worker-pool
resource on the write side that stream_read_timeout_ms protects on the
read side.

max_header_bytes bounds the combined size (request line plus every
header line) of one request's header block; a request whose headers
exceed this is rejected by closing the connection outright (the header
block could not be parsed far enough to send a well-formed error
response), matching every other pre-routing parse error this module's
request parser reports.

max_connections bounds how many connections this listener may have
open simultaneously; once at capacity, new connections are simply left
pending in the kernel's own listen backlog (accept(2) is not called
again until a connection closes and frees a slot) rather than accepted
and immediately rejected.

enable_keepalive sets SO_KEEPALIVE on every accepted connection.
enable_reuseport sets SO_REUSEPORT on the listening socket, letting
multiple chttpsvr instances (e.g. one per worker process) bind the
identical host:port for kernel-load-balanced accept. ipv6_only sets
IPV6_V6ONLY on an AF_INET6 listener, so it does not also implicitly
accept IPv4-mapped connections. All three are applied best-effort (a
setsockopt failure does not fail chttpsvr_start) and have no effect on
a Unix domain socket listener.

Use CHTTPSVR_QUEUE_UNBOUNDED for worker_queue_capacity to create a pool
with no capacity limit.

Override individual fields after applying the macro:

  chttpsvr_config_t cfg = CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT;
  cfg.port = 9090;
  cfg.worker_thread_count = 8;
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR CHTTPSVR_QUEUE_UNBOUNDED (3)
